\begin_body

\begin_layout Title
Public Economics I: Class Presentation 1
\end_layout

\begin_layout Author
Nathan Smooha
\end_layout

\begin_layout Date
October 24, 2013
\end_layout

\begin_layout Abstract
Stinebrickner, Todd R.
 and Ralph Stinebrickner (2008) 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

The Causal Effect of Studying on Academic Performance,
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 
\emph on
The B.E.
 Journal of Economic Analysis & Policy
\emph default
: Vol.
 8: Iss.
 1 (Frontiers), Article 14.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Introduction
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Little is known about the causual impact of a student's study effort on
 educational outcomes for two reasons:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
Data sources have not traditionally collected information about how much
 time students spend studying
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
The amount of time a person studies is endogenously determined, a sample
 correlation between study quantity and grade performance may not represent
 a useful estimator of the causual effect of stuyding.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Authors take advantage of a useful institutional detail at Berea College
 and unique new data from the Berea Panel Study (BPS) that they collected
 specifically for the purposes of this paper.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The identification strategy relies heavily on the fact that Berea assigns
 first-year roommates using a mechanism that is unconditionally random,
 so that the academic ability, motivation, and all other characteristics
 of a student at the time of assignmnet are uncorrelated with the characteristic
s of his/her roommate.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
The authors are able to partition students into groups that are identical
 in all respects at the time of college entrance except that the students
 in the different groups are assgined roommates of different observable
 type.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Thus, if one could observe the grade inputs of students that are determined
 after the time of college entrance and are influenced by the obervable
 type of a student's roommate, then one could estimate the causal effect
 of these inputs on academic performance.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Two potential difficulties that would typically render the identification
 strategy infeasible:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
It is not trivial to find observable characteristics of roommates that can
 be used to generate student groups which differ in terms of college grades.
 Past research has found little evidence that a student's grade performance
 is related to observable academic characteristics of his roommate.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Solution: 
\series default
In the short-run, a student's ability is to a large extent, fixed, but that
 the student's decisions about time-use may be influenced by peers.
 The BPS was used to collect information about a student's roommate that
 could possibly influence the student's time-use.
 For example, of central importance to the paper, the BPS reveals whether
 a student's first year roommate brought a video game with him/her to school
 at the beginning of the academic year.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
Standard data sources typically do not provide information about the amount
 that student studies or the other grade inputs that are potentially determined
 after the time of college entrance and, therefore, are potentially influenced
 by roommates.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Solution: 
\series default
The authors included in the BPS multiple 24-hour time diaries and additional
 survey questions that allowed them to characterize study quantity and other
 college choices and behaviors that could influence grade performance.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
In Section 3, the author's estimate the reduced-form equation.
 They determine whether a student's roommate brings a video game to school
 has a strong causal effect on the student's grade performance.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Important inputs in the grade production function can be influenced after
 college.
 Consequently, grade perofrmance is far from predetermined at the time of
 college entrance.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Section 4 examines what inputs are responsible for difference in average
 grade performance between students whose roommates bring video games and
 students whose roommates do not bring video games.
 The authors group the set of inputs that could be influenced by the presence
 of the roommate video game into three broad categories: class attendance,
 study quantity, and study efficiency.
 
\series bold
\emph on
(What about forming study groups, seeking tutoring help, visiting professors
 during office hours, etc.?)
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Class attendance not influenced by the presence of a roommate video game
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Authors find evidence of substantial differences in study quantity between
 the groups, but no evidence of any differences in study efficiency.
 
\series bold
\emph on
(I find this result hard to believe because it is impossible to measure
 every possible measure of 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

study efficiency.
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 Specific study habits and techniques have a direct influence on study efficienc
y, and I do not believe that the authors have accounted for this.)
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
The authors formally quantify the causal effect of study quantity on academic
 performance with an IV strategy.
 
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
The presence of a roommate video game serves as an instrument for a student's
 study quantity.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The primary identification assumption is that only effect the IV has on
 academic performance is through its effect on study quantity, i.e., the presence
 of a roommate video game is uncorrelated with the error term in the properly
 specified equation.
 Also, we must believe that the difference in study efficiency is trivial
 between students whose roommates bring video games and students whose roommates
 do not bring video games.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The difference in average grades between the group of students whose rommates
 bring video games and the group of students whose rommates do not bring
 games is attributed to the difference in average study quantity between
 the two groups.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
IV estimates are much larger than the OLS estimates.
 If the identifying assumption is true, then this suggests that the OLS
 estimates are biased downwards.
 Note that the unobservable in the grade equation caputres both permanent
 attributes of individuals and semester specific influences on grade performance.
 
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Endogeneity problems could arise either because students who spend more
 time studying have different permanent, unobserved attributes such as ability
 or because of a 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

dynamic selection
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 effect in which students who receive bad grade shocks or have difficult
 classes during a particular semester react by chaning their effort during
 that semester.
 
\series bold
\emph on
Are there any other relevant stories to suggest that this may be the case?
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
With respect to the former possibility, the authors find no evidence that
 study quantiy varies with observable permanent meausre of ability.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Yet, they do find evidence that the difference between the IV and OLS estimates
 can be explained by the dynamic selection effect.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
However, one can construct a story to suggest that the IV estimates could
 be positively biased.
 
\series bold
\emph on
For example, students with roommates who have a video game could have their
 unobserved study habits, sleep schedules, etc.
 negatively affected by the presence of this additional distraction.
 Also, I believe some of the unobserved effects of study efficiency have
 still not been considered.
 To elaborate, if one is going to just focus on study quantity, then I expect
 study quantity to have a quadratic influence on academic performance.
 It has a positive effect on academic performance only up to a certain point,
 and then the effect becomes negative.
 Are there any other relevant stories to suggest that the IV estimates may
 be biased?
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Section*
Section 2: A General Overview of the BPS and the Sample
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The identification strategy takes advantage of the fact that students at
 Berea who do not request roommates are unconditionally randomly assigned
 roommates.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The sample used only contains 210 participants from a 2001 cohort who live
 on campus, did not request a roommate, and are not mssing imporant administrati
ve data needed for this paper.
 
\series bold
\emph on
This is a small and incredibly limited sample, especially when we consider
 the fact that slightly more than one-third of students at Berea either
 live off campus or request a roommate
\emph default
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The authors claim the identification strategy is feasible because their
 data contain unique information about a student's roommate that could possibly
 influence the student's time-use
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Descriptive statistics are shown in the first panel of Table 1.
 Information about a student's roommate is shown in the third panel of Table
 1.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The defining indicator variable 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{RGAME}_{i}$
\end_inset

 is equal to one if student 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

's roommate brought either a 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

video game
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 or a 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

computer game.
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 They also show results when the variable is disaggregated into its two
 separate categories.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The authors assert a second reason for the feasibility of the identifcation
 strategy.
 The BPS contains mutiple 24-hour time diaries and additional survery questions
 that allow them to characterize the amount that students study and a long
 list of other college choices and behaviors that we could imagine influencing
 grade performance.
 
\series bold
\emph on
The information they obtain from their survery could possibily be plagued
 by measurement error or false information.
 They even admit that different wording would have allowed them to more
 clearly distinguish between prevailing categories of video games.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Section 3: Differences In Grade Performance By RGAME And Other Variables
 (Instruments)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The random assignment of roommates implies that, in the population, the
 distribution of characteristics other than sex is identical for students
 with 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{RGAME}_{i}$
\end_inset

 equal to one and students with 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{RGAME}_{i}$
\end_inset

 equal to zero.
 Thus, conditional on sex, any differences in grade performance between
 the two 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{RGAME}$
\end_inset

 groups in the population can be attributed to differences in inputs that
 are influenced after the time of college entrance.
 
\series bold
\emph on
Why do we condition on sex? Because students are assigned to a roommate
 of the same sex
\series default
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Due to the small sample size, they must pool male and female observations
 and condition on sex.
 In addition, they condition on other observable characteristics.
 
\series bold
\emph on
With random assignment, we expect no differences to exist between 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{RGAME}$
\end_inset

 groups in the population.
 So, why do the authors condition on the other observable characteristics?
 Differences may exist in other observables between 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{RGAME}$
\end_inset

 groups in the sample because of sampling variation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Table 2 shows the estimates of the reduced-form IV equation, in which we
 replace the endogenous variable with the instrument.
 So, the table shows the direct effect of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{RGAME}$
\end_inset

 and other roommate variables (instruments) on GPA.
 The estimate is a large magnitude and it is signficant at levels greater
 than .008.
 Also, this is good evidence that the instrument satisfies the relevance
 condition, i.e., we do not have a weak instrument.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The authors also examine whether the effect of having a roommate who brings
 a video game depends on whether or not the student himself brought a game.
 Table 3 provides the descriptive statistics that are relevant for examining
 whether important interaction effects might be present in our context.
 The findings are formalized further in column 5 of Table 2.
 
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
The interaction effect does not appear to have a significant effect, so
 the authors conclude that 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{RGAME}_{i}$
\end_inset

 does a reasonable job summarizing the useful information for identifying
 the direct effect of video games on 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{GPA}_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
As a result, the when examining inputs that are responsible for differences
 in grade performance, the authors focus the discussion exclusively on differenc
es in grade performance generated by the binary roommate variable 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{RGAME}_{i}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Section*
Section 4: What Inputs Are Responsible for Differences in Grades Between
 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{RGAME}$
\end_inset

 Groups?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The difference in 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{GPA}_{i}$
\end_inset

 between 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{RGAME}$
\end_inset

 groups implies that the presence of the roommate video game affects grade
 inputs that are determined, at least partially, after the time of college
 entrance.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
The identifying assumption relies on the notion that 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{RGAME}$
\end_inset

 does not affect 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{GPA}_{i}$
\end_inset

 through any other input except study quantity.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The presence of a roommate video game could affect a student's grades either
 because the physical game itself influences the student's inputs or because
 roommates who bring games are systematically different in ways that influence
 the student's inputs.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
There is some evidence that the former possibility is important.
 There is a significant difference in the time spend playing video games
 between students who have 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{RGAME}_{i}$
\end_inset

 equal to one and students who have 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{RGAME}_{i}$
\end_inset

 equal to zero.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
With respect to the latter possibility, at least in terms of observable
 characteristics, the authors find no evidence that students who bring video
 games are different than those who do not bring video games.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
The authors identify three broad input categories that affect a student's
 GPA: study quantity, class attendance, and study efficiency.
 In Section 4, the authors examine whether the data provide evidence of
 differences in these three categories by 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{RGAME}$
\end_inset

 group.
 
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
If 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{RGAME}$
\end_inset

 does not have a statistially significant effect on class attendance and
 the variables used to measure study efficiency, then this provides some
 noteworthy evidence that the identifying assumption holds.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
However, in order to obtain promising estimates, we should still see a significa
nt difference in study quantity between the students who have 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{RGAME}_{i}$
\end_inset

 equal to one and students who have 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{RGAME}_{i}$
\end_inset

 equal to zero.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Subsection*
Section 4.A: Class Attendance
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Class attendance is to a large degree mandatory at Berea with many faculty
 members imposing strict attendance policies and faculty typically either
 formally or informally keeping track of the attendance of individual students.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The purpose of Table 4A is to show that 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{RGAME}_{i}$
\end_inset

 does not have a significant affect on class attendance.
 
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Column 1 uses information obtained from the time surverys 
\begin_inset Formula $(\mbox{PATTEND}_{i})$
\end_inset

, and column 2 uses information from the time diaries 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{(CLASSHOURS}_{i})$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
There is no difference in class attendance between students who bring video
 games themselves and those who do not bring video games themselves.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
The authors find no evidence that class attendance varies between 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{RGAME}$
\end_inset

 groups.
 They conclude that studying, defined generally to include both study quantity
 and study efficiency, plays a very important causal role in determining
 grade performance.
 
\series bold
\emph on
However, the validity of the identifying assumption would have been improved
 if the authors would have combined these two inputs together into one variable,
 as opposed to separating out the effects.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Section 4.B: Study Quantity
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Pooling the male and female observations, and conditioning on observable
 characteristics, the authors regress study quantity (the endogenous variable)
 on 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{RGAME}$
\end_inset

 (the instrument), which is equivalent to the first stage regression of
 the 2SLS estimation procedure.
 This is the empirical test to directly verify the relevance condition.
 The results are shown in the first column of Table 5A.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
With respect to the effect of primary interest, an estimate and std.
 error of -.668 and .252, respectively, so that a test of the null hypothesis
 that 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{RGAME}_{i}$
\end_inset

 has no effect on study-effort is rejected at all levels of significance
 greater than .01
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Given that students in the sample study 3.43 hours per day on average, the
 estiamted reduction of approximately two-thirds of an hour per day is quantitat
ively very substantial.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
\emph on
The authors do no provide F-statistics for the full regressions.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Subsection*
Section 4.C: Study Efficiency
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Differences in rest/altertness across 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{RGAME}$
\end_inset

 groups
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The efficiency of a student's study effort depends, in part, on whether
 the student is rested/alert when studying (or in class).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Using the time diaries, the authors construct a variable 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{SLEEP}_{i}$
\end_inset

, which measures the number of hours that a student sleeps per night.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
The third column of Table 4A shows the results from a regression of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{SLEEP}_{i}$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{RGAME}_{i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{MALE}_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 The estimated effect is very small and not statistically significant.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
The authors also use the time diaries to construct a variable 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{BEDTIME}_{i}$
\end_inset

 that indicates the time at which a student goes to bed.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Column 4 of Table 4A shows a regression of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{BEDTIME}_{i}$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{RGAME}_{i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{MALE}_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 There is no evidence that the presence of the roommate video game influences
 a student's bedtime
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Alcohol use may represent another reason that students are not rested/alert
 when studying or in class.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
A story could be told that a student who brings a video game to school is
 more likely to drink alcohol and/or party, which, in turn, could affect
 his/her roommate's study quantity.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Column 4B show a regresson of the number of hours spent partying on 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{MALE}_{i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{RGAME}_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 There is no evidence of a relationship between the number of hours spent
 partying and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{RGAME}_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Also, there is no strong reason to believe, a priori, that students who
 bring video games to school are more likely to drink.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Another possibility is that paid employment may cause students to not be
 rested/alert for their studies.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Institutional details of the school imply that there cannot be substantial
 differences in paid employment by 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{RGAME}$
\end_inset

 group.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Differences in the quality of the physical study location across 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{RGAME}$
\end_inset

 groups
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The efficiency of a student's study effort may also depend on the quality
 of the physical location where the student studies.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Once concern could be that the presence of a video game in the dorm room
 implies that the student may not be able to study in the room when he wants
 to because, for example, the room has become a place where others congregate.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
A related way that studying might be less efficient for students who have
 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{RGAME}_{i}$
\end_inset

 equal to one would be if the video game or the television that may accompany
 the video game serves as a distraction while the student is studying.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The presence of the roommate video game could potentially imporve the physical
 environment of a room in some cases.
 For example, treated students may be more likely to have a computer in
 their room and this could represent an academic advantage for treated students.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
In Column 1 of Table 4B, the percentage of study time that takes place in
 the dorm room is regressed on 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{RGAME}_{i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{MALE}_{i}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
The estimated effect of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{RGAME}_{i}$
\end_inset

 is not statistically significant.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
There is no difference in study locations for those who have 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{RGAME}_{i}$
\end_inset

 equal to one and those who have 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{RGAME}_{i}$
\end_inset

 equal to zero.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Column 2 of Table 4B shows no effect of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{RGAME}_{i}$
\end_inset

 in a regression with the percentage of time spent studying with the television
 on as the dependent variable and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{RGAME}_{i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{MALE}_{i}$
\end_inset

 as independent variables.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
\emph on
The authors admit it is hard to know for sure whether a person would answer
 that he was studying witht the television on if his roommate was playing
 a video game on the television.
 However, the authors think this is not a serious problem since a small
 fraction of a student's study time would, on average, take place with the
 video game on.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
In Column 3 of Table 4B, the authors regress the number of hours per week
 that a student uses a computer for academic reasons on 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{RGAME}_{i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{MALE}_{i}$
\end_inset

 
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
The estimated effect of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{RGAME}_{i}$
\end_inset

 is not statistically significant.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Differences in academic assistance from peers across 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{RGAME}$
\end_inset

 groups
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The possibility that students whose roommates bring video games are studying
 less efficiently could also be of relevance if these students have roommates
 who are less able or less willing to help them directly with their coursework.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
In an earlier paper the authors had written, they concluded that, in the
 short-run, peer effects are much more likely to be transmitted by good
 role models influencing the time-use decisions of their roommates than
 by high ability students helping their roommates understand their coursework.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
In the data, there is no relationship between the 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{RGAME}$
\end_inset

 variable and the amount of time a student spends interacting with his roommate
 on academic matters.
 Also, there is no evidence that students with 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{RGAME}_{i}$
\end_inset

 equal to one have roommates who would be lessa ble to provide academic
 assistance than students with 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{RGAME}_{i}$
\end_inset

 equal to zero.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Subsection*
