\begin_layout Subsection*
Section 5B: Instrumental variables estimators
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
There are three potential biases present in the OLS estimator
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{STUDY}_{i}^{*}$
\end_inset

 could be correlated with the error term because students who spend more
 time studying may be different in permanent, unobserved ways than students
 who spend less time studying.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{STUDY}_{i}^{*}$
\end_inset

 could be correlated with the error term because students may react to semester-
specific factors in the error term by chaning how much they study.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
An errors-in-variables problem is created when 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{STUDY}_{i}^{*}$
\end_inset

 is replaced by its observable but noisy proxy 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{STUDY}_{i}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
IV estimation represents a theoretically appealing way to deal with the
 potential problems listed above.
 What is needed is a variable that has a direct effect on the amount that
 a person studies (i.e., satisfies a relevance condition), but is uncorrelated
 witht he unobservable determinants of grade performance as captured by
 the error term (i.e., satisfies an exogeneity condition).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The random assignment of roommates guarantees that 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{RGAME}_{i}$
\end_inset

 is unrelated to all elements of the error term, such as unobserved ability,
 that are predetermined at the time of college entrance.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
The three avenues identified by the authros through which 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{RGAME}_{i}$
\end_inset

 could potentially influence grade performance are study quantity, class
 attendance, and study efficiency.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
For the IV to be valid, 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{RGAME}_{i}$
\end_inset

 must influence study quantity (relevance), but it does not affect either
 class attendance or study efficiency (exogeneity).
 Evidence that these two conditions are safistied was provided in Section
 4.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
\emph on
Due to random assignment, we are confident that this instrument is exogenous
 to the error term.
 So, why might the identifying assumption fail? The instrument could indirectly
 affect outcomes through its influence on other unobserved inputs.
 It seems somewhat implausible that 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{RGAME}_{i}$
\end_inset

 does not affect study efficiency.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
With a binary instrument, we can emply a straightforward Wald estimator
 which attributes the difference in 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{GPA}_{i}$
\end_inset

 between the two 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{RGAME}$
\end_inset

 groups to the difference in average study quantity between the two groups.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Table 7A provides formal IV estimates, with each column representing the
 utilization of a different IV.
 Table 7B provides formal IV estiamtes using non-game instruments.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
\emph on
Estimates and standard errors change considerably when different IVs are
 implemented.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
\emph on
The authors do not include a typical robustness check found in most IV papers:
 testing to see if there is selection on observables.
 The authors only provide estimates when all the controls are included in
 the regression; they do not provide estimates for the simple IV case, which
 is bad form.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
\emph on
Do the estimates change signficantly when we add control variables to the
 baseline model? If there is a signficant change, then this provides evidence
 that there is selection on unobservables, so the identifying assumption
 is probably invalid.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
To provide additional support that the results were not being drive by differenc
es (between the treated and untreated groups) in behaviors other than study
 effort, the authors also estimated a specification which added as regressors
 all of the variables in Table 4A and Table 4B.
 They found that the results changed very little, even when adding the explanato
ry variable 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{OGAME}_{i}$
\end_inset

, which indicates whehter the student himself brought a video game.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
